 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "A compound is a pure substance made of two or more elements chemically joined in a fixed ratio. Its properties are different from those of its elements: sodium (a soft, dangerous metal) and chlorine (a poisonous gas) join to make sodium chloride, the salt we eat.\nA formula shows which elements are in a compound and how many atoms of each. The small number after a symbol counts the atoms just before it; no number means one.\n• H₂O (water): 2 hydrogen atoms, 1 oxygen atom.\n• CO₂ (carbon dioxide): 1 carbon, 2 oxygen.\n• CaCO₃ (calcium carbonate): 1 calcium, 1 carbon, 3 oxygen.\nA compound can only be split into its elements by a chemical reaction.",
  "checks": [
   "atomCount('CaCO3', 'O') == 3",
   "totalAtoms('H2O') == 3"
  ]
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "f": "H2SO4"
  },
  "problem": "Sulfuric acid has the formula {formula(f)}. Which elements does it contain, and how many atoms of each?",
  "steps": [
   "Read the symbols: {symbolsIn(f)}, that is {elementNames(f)}.",
   "The small numbers: H has {atomCount(f, 'H')}, S has {atomCount(f, 'S')} (no number means one), O has {atomCount(f, 'O')}.",
   "Total: {atomCount(f, 'H')} + {atomCount(f, 'S')} + {atomCount(f, 'O')} = {totalAtoms(f)} atoms."
  ],
  "answer": "{elementNames(f)}: {atomList(f)} ({totalAtoms(f)} atoms in all).",
  "checks": [
   "totalAtoms(f) == 7"
  ]
 },
